\title{Queries and Views}
\import{prelude}

\p{So far, we have built types that describe database schemas. But we can us all the same constructors to describe queries on these databases.}

\p{To declare queries (and more general things), we use the following syntax. This is close to the syntax used for declaring theories themselves, but we allow ourselves to specify the output type of our declaration.}

\pre{%
\kw{def} my-def : X := ...
}

\p{Like theories, we allow these declarations to take parameters.}

\pre{%
\kw{def} my-def (x : X) (y : Y) : Z := ...
}

\p{Now, the actual notion of query we will be using:}

\subtree[001A]{
  \taxon{Slogan}
  \title{Sets are queries, elements are results}

  \p{A \em{query} on a theory is a way of turning a model of the theory (i.e. actual database instance) into a set. Applying this to a particular model gives an actual set, and elements of that set are the results of the query.}
}

\p{That is, a query on some theory \code{T} is just definition that has the following shape:}

\pre{%
\kw{def} my-query (m : T) : \kw{Set} := ...
}

\p{Here are some simple cases. Given database containing a graph, we might want to query for the full set of vertices. For this, we can project the field of the model directly.}

\pre{%
\kw{theory} Graph := \kw{sig}
  V : Set
  E : V -> V -> Set
\kw{end}

\kw{def} its-vertices (m : Graph) : \kw{Set} := m.V
}

\p{Or, to constrain the results according to the value of a column, we might apply a function that returns a set:}

\pre{%
\kw{theory} Layered := \kw{sig}
  layers : Int -> Set
\kw{end}

\kw{def} for-millenium (m : Layered) : \kw{Set} := m.layers 2000
}

\p{For more complicated queries, we will have to use the constructors we've seen.}

\transclude{001J}

\mvrnote{Views}

\transclude{001K}

\transclude{001L}
